{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>וַיַּפְשִׁיטוּ אֶת יוֹסֵף וְגוֹ׳.</b> According to the plain sense of the verse, its saying <b>כֻּתָּנְתּוֹ</b> refers to the undergarment, and <b>כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים</b> is the outer garment that his father made for him. According to this, it should have preceded with the stripping of the outer garment, which is כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים. And to understand the verse's intent, there is a further fine point to examine: why did it not say ״וְאֶת כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים״?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the verse's intent is that the brothers never wanted to strip him of his undergarment and leave him naked, but only to remove from upon him כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים, which was the source of their jealousy toward him. And the verse informs us that when they removed כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים, out of great anger and hatred, they were not careful to remove it gently and calmly, but with great hatred and such cruelty that they also removed with it the undergarment — that is, the כֻּתֶּנֶת. And this is the verse's measure: <b>וַיַּפְשִׁיטוּ אֶת יוֹסֵף</b> — meaning, nothing remained but Yosef's naked body, for they removed his כֻּתֶּנֶת, which is the undergarment, together with כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים when they removed it from upon him without intent." "And its saying <b>אֲשֶׁר עָלָיו</b> intends to magnify the matter, for כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים was the outer garment, and they removed all the garments and also the undergarment, all of them together with it, out of the abundance of anger and hatred."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:45.779331089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}